  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max reflects on the belief that thoughts are material: if you think about something constantly, it is bound to come true. In the middle of the night, his phone rings and pregnant Vika asks him to bring her canned stewed meat.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Говорят, что мысли материальны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если о чём-то постоянно думать, это обязательно произойдёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Алло, Вика?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Алло, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, что-то случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, не молчи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Можешь мне кое-что привезти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что, лекарства?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тушёнку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Привези мне тушёнку, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Говорят, что мысли материальны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They say that thoughts are material.' Max anchors the line on 'say', while 'material' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если о чём-то постоянно думать, это обязательно произойдёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If you think about something constantly, it will definitely happen.' The grammar packs 'if' and 'to happen' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Алло, Вика?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Vika?' Max raises the pitch on 'Vika' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Алло, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Max.' In Russian, the idea of 'hello' flows naturally into 'Max' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, что-то случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, did something happen?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yes' and finishing on 'happened'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, не молчи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, do not stay silent.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'be silent'. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Можешь мне кое-что привезти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can you bring me something?' Vika anchors the line on 'can', while 'to bring' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, of course!' Max brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'of course' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что, лекарства?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is it, medicine?' Notice how Russian expresses 'what' and 'medicines' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тушёнку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Canned stewed meat.' The grammar packs 'canned stewed meat' and 'canned stewed meat' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What?' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'what' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Привези мне тушёнку, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bring me canned stewed meat, please.' In Russian, the idea of 'to bring' flows naturally into 'please' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
